2. Cash-trip debt collection background process

It works, but only partially. The automation chain is:

Cash trip → recordCommissionDebt() ✓ (writes debt, sets RESTRICTED if over threshold)
  ↓
checkDebt endpoint: blocks RESTRICTED drivers from new rides ✓
  ↓
AutoSettlementService.autoSettleDriverDebts() ← @Cron(EVERY_HOUR)
     └── deducts outstanding debt from POSTED (pending payout) driver earnings ✓
     └── updates GL: DR 2302 Driver Payable / CR 1403 Driver Debt Receivable ✓
What's missing (no code exists):

Wallet top-up trigger — loading a wallet does NOT auto-deduct outstanding debt
Reminders — no SMS, email, push for unpaid debts
Interest/penalties — no late fees or accruals on overdue debts
Only POSTED earnings — skips PENDING (un-journalized) earnings
So if a driver has debt and gets future earnings (from more trips), the hourly cron will deduct from them. But if a driver directly loads wallet cash, the debt sits untouched.

3. Are real test money evenly distributed?

No real money moves at all. The only working distribution path is wallet-pay, which moves internal ledger entries only:

DR rider wallet (2101) 
→ CR unsettled (2402)
   → DR unsettled (2402)
      → CR platform commission revenue (4201)
      → CR driver earnings payable (2302) 
         → DR driver earnings payable (2302)
            → CR driver wallet (2101)
This is double-entry accounting — correct on paper, but no real NGN leaves the system. For card payments (initiate → webhook → complete), every Paystack transfer call falls to mock because the key lacks permissions.

To test with real money distribution: The user needs to upgrade the Paystack test key (enable Transfers/Subaccounts in dashboard), replace the hardcoded platform bank account with a real one, and use real test bank details for drivers.